<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persian Gulf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Persian Gulf is an arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> by the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>. Since antiquity it has carried trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. It was vital to the commerce of empires from the Akkadian and Babylonian to the Sasanian, Abbasid and Safavid, and the Afsharid and Zand rulers sought to control its ports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-gulf.jpg" alt="Persian Gulf" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PersianGulf_vue_satellite_du_golfe_persique.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliphate's revenues derived from agricultural taxation across Mesopotamia's highly productive Sawad, customs duties on the Indian Ocean trade passing through the <b>Persian Gulf</b>, and remittances from provincial governors across an empire stretching from North Africa to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Zeila's revenues derived from the trade in slaves, ivory, gold, coffee and livestock moving between the East African interior and the markets of Arabia, India and the <b>Persian Gulf</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Persian Gulf</b> trade through Bushehr and Bandar Abbas continued, and Nader's intervention in Oman, culminating in the capture of Muscat around 1743, extended Iranian reach into the Arabian Sea, but these revenues were insufficient to sustain an empire held together by military force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the course of what his inscriptions describe as 34 battles, Sargon brought the Sumerian city-states of southern Mesopotamia under Akkadian control, symbolically washing his weapons in the <b>Lower Sea</b>, the Persian Gulf, after the conquest of the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cilician Armenia grew rich on its position at the junction of routes linking Central Asia and the <b>Persian Gulf</b> to the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade connected Babylon to the <b>Persian Gulf</b> routes that brought copper from Dilmun and Magan, along with stone and timber, compensating for Mesopotamia's lack of these materials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
